I still think this will be an issue. Consider the following example:
```
// Frame 0
// I record commands to frames[frame].draw_command_buffer
// Frame 1
// I record more commands to frames[frame].draw_command_buffer
// I call submit()
```
Before this PR ``frame`` is never increased, so it references the same draw_command_buffer in both cases. With this PR, we will record to both frames[0] and frames[1] and then only submit [1]. Even worse, we could record commands to frame 0, submit on 1, and sync on 3. Then we will end up doing nothing and leaving the command buffers partially recorded.

I still think this will be an issue. Consider the following example:
```
// Frame 0
// I record commands to frames[frame].draw_command_buffer
// Frame 1
// I record more commands to frames[frame].draw_command_buffer
// I call submit()
```
Before this PR ``frame`` is never increased, so it references the same draw_command_buffer in both cases. With this PR, we will record to both frames[0] and frames[1] and then only submit [1]. Even worse, we could record commands to frame 0, submit on 1, and sync on 3. Then we will end up doing nothing and leaving the command buffers partially recorded.

If what you're asking is (ignoring performance) if we could wait here doing:

`vkWaitForFences(device, 1, &fences[(frame_index + 1u) % frame_count], VK_TRUE, UINT64_MAX);`

Then the answer is yes, that would be functionally equivalent.

Now if we consider performance, you're asking the comparison:

```cpp
// What you propose
wait( A );
submit( B );

// vs 

// What I wrote
submit( B );
wait( A );
```

In both cases we wait for A. But in my version by the time we're done with the CPU code submission, the GPU may already be done and the wait won't happen.

In fact we should delay `wait( A )` as much as possible (in order to lower the chance we even wait at all). It's just that due to the current complexity of Godot, the current place where I've placed it is the safest spot that I could delay it.

When would we be forced to wait (i.e. cannot delay any longer)?

 - We need to destroy a vulkan handle (e.g. a framebuffer, render pass, possibly a shader & pso if they were phased out N frames ago).
 - We need to map a buffer for reading or writing.
 - We need to reuse (reset) a command buffer for preparing commands.

